# The firmware the Mac image (variants "mac" and "macweb") keeps. post-build.sh deletes every other file under /lib/firmware
# for those variants; the "hw" image keeps the whole linux-firmware set of iyakki_firmware.fragment. Why: GRUB reads the
# initramfs through the Mac firmware's own USB driver at about 6 MB/s, so every compressed megabyte here is about 0.17 s of
# a silent logo at every start (docs/MAC-HARDWARE.md, "Boot time").
#
# One pattern per line, relative to /lib/firmware; shell globbing, where * also matches '/'. A kept symbolic link also keeps
# the file it points to. Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are ignored. crates/iyakki-core/tests/boot_menu.rs checks
# that the files Macs need (the names below marked "required") stay matched.
#
# What is deliberately NOT kept: Intel Wi-Fi and Bluetooth (iwlwifi, intel/ibt: no Mac has them), Qualcomm ath10k/ath12k,
# Realtek rtw88/rtw89/rtlwifi (no Mac, and no adapter docs/MAC-HARDWARE.md recommends), Broadcom SDIO and Cypress files
# (single-board computers), bnx2/bnx2x/ice/e100 (server and old PCI NICs), Marvell mwifiex, the GPUs of other PCs. A Mac
# with such a card can use the "hw" image or a driver pack (Store, Drivers).

# Wireless regulatory database (every Wi-Fi driver).
regulatory.db
regulatory.db.p7s

# Intel graphics (i915): Skylake (MacBook 2016, iMac 2015/2017), Kaby Lake and Amber Lake (MacBook Pro 2017, MacBook Air
# 2018-2019), Coffee Lake (Mac mini 2018, MacBook Pro 2018-2020: it uses the kbl_ files), Comet Lake (iMac 2020), Ice Lake
# (MacBook Air 2020, MacBook Pro 13" 2020 four ports). Haswell and Broadwell (Mac mini 2014, MacBook Air 2014-2017) need no
# firmware. All versions of each file are kept so a kernel update that asks for another version still finds it.
# required: i915/kbl_dmc_ver1_04.bin i915/skl_dmc_ver1_27.bin i915/icl_dmc_ver1_09.bin i915/kbl_guc_70.1.1.bin
i915/skl_*
i915/kbl_*
i915/cml_*
i915/icl_*

# AMD graphics (amdgpu). Tonga: iMac 5K 2014/2015 (R9 M295X/M395X). Polaris 10/11/12: Radeon Pro 450-580(X) in MacBook Pro
# 2016-2019 and iMac 2017-2019, Mac Pro 2019 (580X). Vega 10: iMac Pro, iMac 2019 Vega 48; Vega 12: MacBook Pro 2018-2019
# Vega 16/20; Vega 20: Mac Pro 2019 Vega II. Navi 10/12/14: Radeon Pro 5300M/5500M (navi14), 5600M (navi12), iMac 2020 and
# Mac Pro W5500X/W5700X. Navi 21/23 (sienna_cichlid, dimgrey_cavefish): Mac Pro W6800X/W6900X/W6600X.
# Tahiti/Pitcairn/Verde are only used by amdgpu with amdgpu.si_support=1 (radeon drives them by default, below).
# required: amdgpu/polaris11_mc.bin amdgpu/vega12_gpu_info.bin amdgpu/navi14_gpu_info.bin amdgpu/vega10_gpu_info.bin
amdgpu/tonga_*
amdgpu/polaris10_*
amdgpu/polaris11_*
amdgpu/polaris12_*
amdgpu/vega10_*
amdgpu/vega12_*
amdgpu/vega20_*
amdgpu/navi10_*
amdgpu/navi12_*
amdgpu/navi14_*
amdgpu/sienna_cichlid_*
amdgpu/dimgrey_cavefish_*
amdgpu/tahiti_*
amdgpu/pitcairn_*
amdgpu/verde_*

# radeon (Southern Islands): Mac Pro 2013 (FirePro D300 Pitcairn, D500/D700 Tahiti), MacBook Pro 15" 2015 (R9 M370X Cape
# Verde), iMac 2014/2015 (R9 M290X Pitcairn, M380/M390 Cape Verde/Pitcairn).
# required: radeon/verde_mc.bin radeon/pitcairn_smc.bin radeon/TAHITI_uvd.bin
radeon/tahiti_*
radeon/TAHITI_*
radeon/pitcairn_*
radeon/PITCAIRN_*
radeon/verde_*
radeon/VERDE_*
radeon/si58_mc.bin

# Broadcom Wi-Fi on PCIe (brcmfmac): BCM43602 (MacBook Pro 2015-2017, iMac 2015-2017), BCM4350 (MacBook 12" 2015-2017) and
# the other PCIe chips of that family; the old brcmsmac files (BCM43224/4313, MacBook Air 2010-2011).
# required: brcm/brcmfmac43602-pcie.bin brcm/brcmfmac4350-pcie.bin
brcm/brcmfmac*-pcie*
brcm/bcm43xx*

# Broadcom Ethernet (tg3: BCM57766 in the Mac mini 2014 and iMac, BCM57762 in the Thunderbolt Ethernet adapter): tigon/* is NOT kept any
# more. LICENCE.tigon is a two-line "hexadecimal or equivalent format" permission; the owner's rule is that what we are not sure we may
# redistribute is not shipped (docs/RESTRICTED-FIRMWARE.md). tg3 works without it at reduced function (no Energy-Efficient Ethernet on the
# BCM57766); the "Broadcom Tigon3 network firmware" tile of Store -> Setup -> Hardware installs it at the person's click.

# Realtek Ethernet over USB (r8152: RTL8153/RTL8156, LICENCE.rtlwifi_firmware.txt: kept). The PCIe files (r8169: RTL8111/RTL8125 in a
# Thunderbolt or USB-C dock, LICENSE.r8169) are NOT kept: same rule, the "Realtek network firmware" tile installs them.
# required: rtl_nic/rtl8153b-2.fw rtl_nic/rtl8156b-2.fw
rtl_nic/rtl8153*
rtl_nic/rtl8156*

# USB Wi-Fi adapters recommended in docs/MAC-HARDWARE.md section 4: Atheros AR9271 (ath9k_htc). The MediaTek ones (MT7612U, MT7921AU, MT7601U) are
# NOT kept: MediaTek's licence is not an open-source one, so the "MediaTek Wi-Fi and Bluetooth firmware" tile (fw-mediatek) installs them. Also the tiny files of other common USB adapters: AR7010 (ath9k_htc), AR9170
# (the old ar9170 files only; carl9170-1.fw is GPL without a source offer in linux-firmware, so it is NOT kept: a tile installs it),
# Ralink RT2870/RT3070/RT5370 (rt2800usb).
# required: ath9k_htc/htc_9271-1.4.0.fw
ath9k_htc/*
htc_9271.fw
htc_7010.fw
ar9271.fw
ar7010*.fw
ar9170-*.fw
rt2870.bin
rt3070.bin
rt3071.bin

# Realtek USB Bluetooth dongles (RTL8761B/BU, e.g. the common 5.0 dongles): the Mac's own Broadcom Bluetooth firmware cannot
# be shipped (section 11), so a dongle is the way to Bluetooth before a driver pack is installed.
rtl_bt/rtl8761b*

# ---- Restricted firmware (docs/RESTRICTED-FIRMWARE.md): never shipped, whatever a pattern above says. post-build.sh also strips every file of
# restricted-firmware.table from the image (all variants), so this list and the table cannot drift apart: a test runs both.
!tigon/*
!bnx2/*
!bnx2x/*
!carl9170-1.fw
!qca/cmbtfw*
!qca/cmnv*
!qca/apbtfw*
!qca/apnv*
!qca/htbtfw*
!qca/htnv*
!qca/hmtbtfw*
!qca/hmtnv*
!qca/QCA6698/*
